class:: PlateDF
summary:: Dragonfly plate reverb UGen
categories:: UGens>Effects
related:: Guides/DragonFlyReverb, Classes/EarlyReflectionsDF, Classes/HallDF, Classes/RoomDF

Description::

Native SuperCollider port of Michael Willis's Dragonfly Plate Reverb. This UGen incorporates three distinct modified Freeverb3 plate algorithms (simple `nrev`, nested `nrevb`, and tank `strev`) to simulate the metallic density and bright decay of physical plate reverberators. It runs the exact same DSP core as the upstream VST3 plugin.

Unlike room algorithms which emulate geometric acoustic reflections, plate algorithms focus on an extremely rapid buildup of echo density and a characteristically smooth, slightly metallic decay. This makes them ideal for percussive sounds, vocals, and any source where a bright, floating reverberation tail is desired without the clutter of early room reflections.

classmethods::

method::ar

argument::inL
Left input.

argument::inR
Right input.

argument::dry
Dry level in percent (`0..100`). Controls the volume of the unaffected input signal.

argument::wet
Wet level in percent (`0..100`). Controls the overall volume of the plate reverberation.

argument::algorithm
Plate algorithm selector (`0` = simple, `1` = nested, `2` = tank). Selects the internal topology of the reverberator.
list::
## `0` (simple `nrev`): A classic Schroeder-style network of allpass and comb filters, producing a smooth and traditional metallic plate sound.
## `1` (nested `nrevb`): A variation with a nested allpass topology, resulting in a denser and thicker reverberant wash.
## `2` (tank `strev`): A tank-like delay topology that introduces a slightly more resonant, metallic spring-like character.
::

argument::width
Stereo width in percent (`50..150`). Widens or narrows the stereo image of the reverb tail.

argument::predelay
Predelay in milliseconds (`0..100`). The delay before the plate reverberation begins, useful for separating transients (like snare hits) from the reverb wash.

argument::decay
Decay time in seconds (`0.1..10`). The time it takes for the mid-band reverberation to fade to silence.

argument::lowCut
Low cut frequency in Hz (`0..200`). Applies a high-pass filter to the reverb tail to remove low-frequency rumble, keeping the plate sounding bright and clean.

argument::highCut
High cut frequency in Hz (`1000..16000`). Applies a static low-pass filter to the tail to simulate general high-frequency absorption.

argument::damp
Damping frequency in Hz (`1000..16000`). Controls the frequency above which the decay time is artificially accelerated. Lower damping frequencies simulate a physical plate under heavier mechanical tension/damping, resulting in a darker tail that decays quickly in the high frequencies.

Examples::

code::
(
{
	// Synthetic snare
	var src = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.01, 0.2), Impulse.ar(1)) * 0.5;
	
	// Synthetic high-bpm hi-hat:
	// var src = HPF.ar(WhiteNoise.ar, 8000) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.05), Impulse.ar(8)) * 0.5;
	
	// For live input instead:
	// var src = SoundIn.ar(0);
	
	PlateDF.ar(
		inL: src,
		inR: src,
		dry: 80.0,
		wet: 40.0,
		algorithm: 1, // 0 = simple, 1 = nested, 2 = tank
		decay: 1.5,
		damp: 6000.0
	);
}.play;
)
::
